{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking initiative to decriminalize drugs will end on Sunday, replaced by a law that re-criminalizes small amounts of controlled substances. The new legislation, HB4002, introduces penalties for possessing substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Offenders can either face misdemeanor charges—carrying up to six months in jail—or opt for treatment programs, including behavioral health interventions and deflection initiatives, to avoid fines. The law also strengthens penalties for selling drugs near sensitive locations, such as parks.\n\nThe recriminalization measure emphasizes police enforcement of public drug use while promoting treatment alternatives. Counties are encouraged, though not required, to develop diversion programs tailored to local resources. Supporters argue this flexibility ensures tailored solutions, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent policies across different regions. The law’s structure leaves room for counties to design their own approaches based on available services.\n\nPolice face tight timelines for implementing the law’s deflection provisions. Officers must determine eligibility—excluding cases with additional charges or violent behavior—within minutes. If a mobile health team cannot respond within 30 minutes, the individual will be jailed. Portland’s police chief stressed the urgency of these constraints, noting that delays could undermine the process.\n\nAdvocates highlight the law’s narrow deflection criteria, which may limit its reach. Only those without prior warrants or violent incidents qualify, raising concerns about accessibility for marginalized groups.\n\nP.P.S Include keywords ['age', 'country', 'eat', 'shopping', 'tongue']", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.0029478687793016434}, {"tok